        '4. Cyanophore Glycosides',
        'Cyanophore Glycosides are a class of glycosides characterized by their ability to yield hydrocyanic acid',
        'among their hydrolysis products.',
        'Several important crude drugs contain cyanophore glycosides. Let\'s discuss them in detail:',
        '1. Wild Cherry',
        'Origin: Wild cherry consists of the carefully dried stem bark of Prunus serotina, which belongs to the',
        'family Rosaceae. The term "prunus" refers to the classic name of the prunus tree, while "serotina"',
        'indicates a late or backward flowering and fruiting time.',
        'Chemical Constituents: Wild cherry contains the cyanogenetic glycoside prunasin. Upon',
        'hydrolysis, prunasin yields benzaldehyde, hydrocyanic acid, and one molecule of -D-',
        'glucopyranose. The resinous material present also yields scopoletin, which is a coumarin, upon',
        'hydrolysis.',
        'Uses: Wild cherry is primarily used in syrup form as a flavored vehicle, especially in cough',
        'remedies (preparations). It has also been considered as a sedative expectorant.',
        '2. Bitter Almonds',
        'Chemical Constituents: Bitter almonds contain a significant amount of another cyanophore',
        'glycoside, amygdalin. This glycoside is also found in the kernels of apricot, peaches, and plums.',
        'Amygdalin is described as a gentiobioside of D-(-)-mandelonitrile (cyanohydrin of',
        'benzaldehyde).',
