        '5. Isothiocyanate Glycosides',
        'Isothiocyanate Glycosides are defined by their characteristic property: they yield an isothiocyanate among',
        'the products of their hydrolysis. Two prominent crude drugs that contain isothiocyanate glycosides are',
        'Black Mustard and White Mustard.',
        '1. Black Mustard (Sinapis Nigra, Brown Mustard)',
        'Origin: Black mustard consists of the dried ripe seeds of Brassica nigra. These plants belong to the',
        'family Brassicaceae.',
        'Chemical Constituents:',
        'o Black mustard contains a significant amount of fixed oil (30-35%).',
        'o The principal chemical constituent is the isothiocyanate glycoside known as sinigrin, which',
        'is also referred to as potassium myronate.',
        'o Sinigrin is stored in specific cells of the plant and is accompanied by the hydrolytic enzyme',
        'myrosin, which is found in adjacent cells. Upon the addition of water to the powdered or',
        'crushed seeds, the enzyme myrosin acts upon sinigrin, causing its hydrolysis.',
        'o This hydrolysis process produces allyl isothiocyanate, which is a volatile compound',
        'commonly known as volatile mustard oil.',
        'Uses:',
        'o Black mustard is used as a local irritant and an emetic.',
        'o Externally, it is applied as a rubefacient (causing redness of the skin) and a vesicant',
        '(causing blisters).',
        'o Commercially, it is also widely used as a condiment.',
        '2. White Mustard',
        'Origin: White mustard consists of the dried ripe seeds of Brassica alba. It also belongs to the family',
        'Brassicaceae.',
        'Chemical Constituents:',
        'o White mustard contains a glucoside called sinalbin.',
