        '6. Flavonoid Glycosides',
        'Flavonoid Glycosides are a class of glycosides characterized by having a flavonoid as their aglycone (the',
        'non-sugar component) in their chemical structure. Flavonoid Glycosides are a type of O-glycoside,',
        'indicating that their sugar and non-sugar moieties are linked through an oxygen atom.',
        'Rutin and Hesperidin contains flavonoid glycosides.',
        '1. Rutin',
        'Origin/Source: Rutin is a flavonol glycoside that has been isolated from Fagopyrum esculentum',
        '(buckwheat residue) and from various species of Eucalyptus.',
        'Chemical Constituents and Hydrolysis: Upon hydrolysis, rutin yields one molecule of quercetin (a',
        'flavonol), one molecule of glucose, and one molecule of rhamnose.',
        'Uses and Properties:',
        'o Rutin has been used in the treatment of various conditions characterized by capillary',
        'bleeding and capillary fragility.',
        'o Flavonoids, in general, are noted for having very good antioxidant property.',
        '7. Aldehyde Glycosides',
        'Aldehyde Glycosides are a class of glycosides that are characterized by containing an aldehyde as their',
        'aglycone (the non-sugar component) in their chemical structure.',
        'The primary crude drug that contains aldehyde glycosides is Vanilla (Vanilla Beans).',
        '1. Vanilla (Vanilla Beans)',
        'Origin/Source: Vanilla consists of the cured, full-grown unripe fruit of Vanilla planifolia, which',
        'belong to the family Orchidaceae.',
        'Commercial Production:',
        'o The vanilla plant is typically propagated by means of cuttings. It takes approximately 2-3',
        'years for the plant to reach the flowering stage. The flowers are hand-pollinated. After',
        'collection, the fruits are ripened to a yellow color.',
        'o The fruits are then treated by dipping them in warm water and repeatedly sweating',
        'between woollen blankets in the sun during the day and packing in wool-covered boxes at',
        'night.',
        'o This curing process requires about two months, during which the pods lose 70-80% of their',
        'original weight. The cured pods are graded and tied into bundles (approx. 50-70), then',
        'sealed in a container.',
        'Chemical Constituents:',
        'o The primary chemical constituents in green vanilla are two glycosides: glucovanillin',
        '(avenin) and glucovanillic alcohol.',
