        'e Hydrolysis: Upon hydrolysis, salicin yields glucose and salicyl alcohol (saligenin).',
        'e Uses and Properties: Salicin possesses ant rheumatic properties. Its action is very closely related',
        'to salicylic acid, and it is likely oxidized to salicylic acid within the human system.',
        '2. Populin (benzoyl salicin)',
        'e Hydrolysis: Upon hydrolysis, populin yields one molecule of salicyl alcohol, one molecule of benzoic',
        'acid, and one molecule of glucose.',
        '3. Glucovanillic Alcohol',
        'e Origin/Source: Glucovanillic alcohol is a constituent of green vanilla.',
        'e Chemical Constituents and Hydrolysis: It is an alcohol glycoside that, upon hydrolysis, yields',
        'glucose and vanillic alcohol.',
        'e Transformation: Glucovanillic alcohol can be further oxidized to vanillin. Vanillin is the principal',
        'flavoring constituent of the cured vanilla.',
        '9. Phenol Glycosides',
        'Phenol Glycosides are a specific class of glycosides characterized by having a phenol as their aglycone',
        '(the non-sugar component) in their chemical structure.',
        'The primary crude drug that contains phenol glycosides is Uva Ursi (bearberry).',
        'Uva Ursi (Bearberry)',
        'e Origin and Source: Uva Ursi consists of the dried leaf of Arctostaphylos uva-ursi.',
        'Chemical Constituents:',
        'o UvaUrsi contains a significant amount of the phenol glycoside arbutin, ranging from 9-18%.',
        'Arbutin is an example of an O-glycoside.',
        'o Upon hydrolysis, arbutin yields hydroquinone and beta-D-glucose.',
        'PAGE 12',
        'o Besides arbutin, Uva Ursi also contains carilogin, several esters of arbutin, quercetin, and',
        'ursolic acid.',
        'Quercetin itself is a flavonol, which is a type of flavonoid (also containing phenolic',
        'hydroxyl groups).',
        'Uses:',
        'o Uva Ursi is utilized for its diuretic and astringent properties, as urinary antiseptic in',
        'urinary tract infections.',
        'Miscellaneous Glycosides',
        'Miscellaneous Glycosides represent a broad category of glycosides that do not fit neatly into the more',
        'specific classifications based on the chemical nature of their aglycone (such as aldehyde, alcohol, or phenol',
        'glycosides) or the linking atom (O-, S-, C-, N-glycosides).',
        'The two specific crude drugs that fall under the "Miscellaneous Glycosides" category: Gentian and Quassia.',
        '1. Gentian',
        'Origin and Source: Gentian consists of the dried rhizomes and roots of Gentiana lutea.',
        'Chemical Constituents:',
        'o Gentian contains bitter glycosides, notably gentiopicroside (about 2%).',
        'o Other constituents include gentioside, xanthone (gentisin), an alkaloid (gentianine), sugars,',
        'pectin, and fixed oil.',
        'Uses:',
        'o Gentian is used as a bitter tonic.',
        'o It is also employed as stomachic for increasing appetite.',
        '2. Quassia (bitter ash)',
        'Origin and Source: Quassia consists of the dried stem wood of Quassia amara.',
        'o The drug obtained from Picrasma excelsa is commercially known as Jamaica quassia.',
        'o The drug obtained from Quassia amara is known commercially as Surinam quassia.',
        'Chemical Constituents:',
        'o Quassin and neoquassin are found in Surinam quassia.',
        'o Picrasmin (isoquassin) has been isolated from Jamaica quassia.',
        'Uses:',
        'o Quassia is used as an enema for the expulsion of thread worms.',
        'o It is also used as an insecticide in the form of fly poison on fly-paper.',
        'In addition to Gentian and Quassia, Miscellaneous glycosides can also include',
        'Antibiotic glycosides (e.g., neomycin, gentamycin, and streptomycin)',
        'Alkaloid glycosides (e.g., solanin)',
        'Nucleosides, and nucleic acids. Nucleosides and nucleic acids are specifically noted as examples of',
        'N-glycosides, where the sugar and non-sugar moieties are linked through a nitrogen atom.',
