        "# Bacteria",
        "“Bacteria are microscopic single-celled organisms that thrive in diverse environments.”",
        "**Introduction: ** Bacteria are prokaryotes. The entire organism consists of a single cell with a simple internal structure. Unlike eukaryotic DNA, which is neatly packed into a cellular compartment called the nucleus, bacterial DNA floats free, in a twisted thread-like mass called the nucleoid. Bacterial cells also contain separate, circular pieces of DNA called plasmids. Bacteria lack membrane-bound organelles, specialized cellular structures that are designed to execute a range of cellular functions from energy production to the transport of proteins.",
        "On the outside, bacterial cells are generally surrounded by two protective coverings: an outer cell wall and an inner cell membrane. However, certain bacteria, like the mycoplasmas do not have a cell wall at all. Some bacteria may even have a third, outermost, protective layer called the capsule. Lastly, bacterial surfaces can be covered by whip-like extensions: flagella or pili. According to the authors of \"Mims Medical Microbiology, 5th Ed\" (Saunders, 2013), long flagella aid in motility while short pili help bacteria to attach to host surfaces .",
        "### Classification of Bacteria",
        "Bacteria can be classified on the following basis:",
        "On the Basis of Oxygen Requirement",
        "On the Basis of Staining",
        "On the Basis of pH",
        "On the Basis of Temperature",
        "On the Basis of Structure",
        "On the Basis of Osmotic Pressure",
        "On the Basis of Shape",
        "### ON THE BASIS OF O 2 REQUIREMENT",
        "Bacteria are classified on the basis of O 2 requirement as:",
        "Obligate aerobes Bacteria which utilize oxygen for producing ATP are called obligate aerobes **e.g.** Pseudomonas, Mycobacterium",
        "** e.g.** E.coli",
        "Facultative anaerobes Bacteria which can produce ATP both by using oxygen or without using oxygen are called as facultative anaerobes",
        "Aerotolerant anaerobes Bacteria which don’t utilize oxygen for producing ATP but they can protect themselves from reactive oxygen molecules. **e.g.** Streptococcus pyogenes",
        "Obligate anaerobes Bacteria which don’t utilize oxygen for producing ATP and are destroyed in the presence of reative oxygen molecules. **e.g.** Clostridium, bacteriodes",
        "Microaerophiles Bacteria which require oxygen to survive but requires environment containing lower levels of oxygen that are present in the atmosphere **e.g.** Campylobacter",
        "### ON THE BASIS OF STAINING",
        "Bacteria are classified on the basis of staining as:",
        "Gram positive Gram positive bacteria are the bacteria which are stained dark violet or purple with stain (Safranine or Fuschine) **e.g** Streptococcus mutans , Staphylococcus aureus, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Bacillus anthracis, Bacillus subtilis",
        "Gram negative Gram negative bacteria are the bacteria which are stained pink or red with stains (Safranine or Fuschine) **e.g** Escherichia coli, Salmonella typhi, Shigella dysenteriae",
        "### ON THE BASIS OF TEMPERATURE",
        "On the basis of temperature bacteria are classified as:",
        "Psychrophiles Psychrophiles are the bacteria which grow at low temperature **e.g** Bacillus psychrophilus",
        "Psychrotrophs e.g Listeria monocytogenes",
        "Mesophiles Mesophiles are the bacteria which grow at middle or normal temperature range. **e.g.** E.coli",
        "Thermophiles Thermophiles are the bacteria which grow at high temperature (i.e 40ᵒC-70ᵒC) **e.g** Bacillus stearothermophilus",
        "Hyperthermophiles Hyperthermophiles ae bacteria which live in extreme hot environment (i.e above 70ᵒC) **e.g** Sulpholobus",
        "### ON THE BASIS OF STRUCTURE",
        "Bacteria are classified on the basis of structure as:",
        "(a) Centre spore former (b) Terminal spore former (c) Subterminal spore former",
        "Spore former Bacteria which form spores are called spore forming Bacteria. These are further classified as: e.g Bacillus cereus e.g Clostridium tetani e.g Clostridium subterminale",
        "e.g Bacillus anthracis, Streptococcus mutans",
        "Capsuleformer Bacteria which form capsule are called capsule forming bacteria.",
        "(a ) Monotrichous => Bacteria having only single flagella",
        "(b) Amphitrichous => Bacteria having tuft of flagella on both poles of bacteria **e.g** Aquaspirillum serpens",
        "(c) Lophotrichous => Bacteria having a tuft of flagella on one pole of bacteria **e.g** Pseudomona flouresence",
        "(d) Peritrichous => Bacteria having flagella covering its whole body **e.g** Salmonella typhi",
        "(e) Atrichous => Bacteria having no flagella on their body **e.g** spirochetes",
        "Flagella ** e.g** Pseudomona auregenosa",
        "### ON THE BASIS OF pH",
        "Bacteria can be classified into following types on the basis of pH:",
        "Acidophiles Bacteria which live in acidic conditions with pH less than 3 **e.g** Sulpholobus",
        "Neutrophiles Bacteria which live in neutral environment. **e.g** Escherichia",
        "Alkalophiles Bacteria which are capable of surviving in alkaline (8.5-11) environments **e.g** Bacillus alkalophiles",
        "### ON THE BASIS OF OSMOTIC PRESSURE",
        "Bacteria can be classified into following types on the basis of osmotic pressure",
        "Osmotolerant Bacteria which can tolerate high osmolarity e.g S.aureus",
        "Halophiles Bacteria which live in high salt concentrations e.g Helicobacter pylori",
        "### ON THE BASIS OF SHAPE:",
        "Cocci Cocci are round shaped bacteria. These are further classified as: (a) Diplococci e.g. Neisseria gonorrhoreae (b) Streptococci e.g. Streptococcus mutans (c) Sarcina e.g. Sarcina rosea (d) Tetrad e.g. Micrococcus luteus (e) Staphylococci e.g Staphylococcus aureus.",
        "Bacilli Baccill are rod shaped bacteria. These are further classified as: (a) Diplobacilli e.g Moraxella lacunata (b) Coccobacilli e.g Bordtella pertussis",
